this is what the virus causing covid19  looked like when it was first detected  in wuhan china now scientists have  identified a new variant called omicron  it has 30 mutations affecting these  spike proteins which the virus uses to  attach itself to human cells to infect  them this is a clarion call as far as  i'm concerned it does appear that  omicron spreads very rapidly  and can be spread between people who are  double vaccinated scientists in south  africa first sounded the alarm over the  variant as people start to move even  more over the next few weeks this will  be all over it has been detected in a  growing number of locations around the  world prompting governments including  the us to restrict travel from southern  africa and israel to ban entry to all  foreign nationals it's too early to know  how concerned we should be about this  but there are enough signals that this  is something that's important that needs  to be investigated the variant was  declared a variant of concern by the  world health organization the agency  said there could be an increased risk of  covid19 re-infection with omicron  compared to other variants and  authorities say it could be more  transmissible a number of cases have  been linked to south africa where  scientists believe it could have caused  a spike in infections the number of  infections detected per day rose sharply  to nearly 3 000 cases on sunday from  roughly 200 cases just two weeks earlier  this new variant has more than 50  mutations more than 30 of them are in  the spike protein which is the part  through which the virus latches onto the  human cell and while there's a lot of  mutations that we've never seen there's  a bunch that  in previous variants have shown to make  the bearing either more contagious or  allow it to evade parts of the immune  response that was triggered by a vaccine  or by a past infection  the omicron variant has twice as many  mutations as the delta variant a highly  contagious strain that was identified in  india october of last year and became  the dominant form of the virus around  the world now scientists are racing to  investigate how widespread the omicron  variant could be it's hard to know  exactly how much it's spread most cases  don't get diagnosed in an even fewer  number gets sequenced so whatever number  of cases are officially reported or  whatever number of countries that have  officially reported cases that is likely  an underestimate with an efficiently  spreading respiratory virus so i suspect  you'll find more cases in more countries  pfizer moderna and other vaccine makers  said they have plans in place to adapt  their vaccines following the emergence  of omicron beyond tech which developed  one of the most commonly used covid19  vaccines together with pfizer said they  expect to be able to tweak their vaccine  in around 100 days if needed when it  comes to variants when they possess  certain mutations that have already been  associated with certain changes in the  behavior of the virus like the ability  to evade vaccines or monoclonal  antibodies or prior immunity or the  ability to transmit more efficiently  those have to be taken seriously and  have to be investigated because it may  change how we need to respond to or  think about the virus and the trajectory  of the pandemic scientists have also  warned for months that the risk of new  variants is higher where vaccination  rates are lower especially in areas with  people who are immunocompromised  in south africa only about one-fourth of  the population is fully vaccinated it's  nearly 60 percent in the us this i think  is not a coincidence that it arose or  was detected in a place with a low  vaccination level which tells us that  the more vaccination we get into people  the higher the vaccination numbers are  the more resilient we're going to be to  variants like this  [Music]
 

